# Paths exempt from secretscan (gitignore-style globs, one per line).
# Keep this list SHORT and reasoned — every entry is a hole in the boundary.

# The scanners' own unit tests are, by definition, a wall of credential-SHAPED
# fixtures (AWS keys, tokens, private-key headers) whose whole job is to prove
# detection. They are deliberately fictional/example-shaped; scanning them only
# yields noise. The scanner *sources* are NOT exempt — a real key pasted there
# is still caught, minus the few detection-pattern lines marked inline.
tools/test_secretscan.py
tools/test_leakscan.py
# signscan's tests build throwaway git repos with example committer emails and a
# placeholder pubkey shape — fixtures, not credentials. signscan.py itself is NOT
# exempt (its embedded selftest signature lines are marked inline instead).
tools/test_signscan.py

# Harness-native worktrees nest INSIDE the repo (.gitignore:14), so a scan rooted
# at the repo top re-reads a whole second copy of the tree — including this
# repo's deliberate fake-secret fixtures, whose own allow-markers no longer
# match once the scan root moves up a level. 29 false positives, every time, for
# any session that scans from the main checkout while a worktree is live.
# linkscan/sizescan already carried this rule; adding it to the rest closes the
# class rather than the next instance.
.claude/worktrees/
